1
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:04,860
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:06,040
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:11,000
My name is Kathryn and today we've got an upper intermediate level lesson for you all about money.

4
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:11,880
That's right.

5
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:13,640
We are trading money.

6
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:19,580
We're actually negotiating in this very popular topic nowadays, the whole foreign exchange market.

7
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:23,280
Okay, so we're talking about currency, which is the money of different countries.

8
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:25,600
For example, the currency of Ecuador is?

9
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:26,280
The US dollar.

10
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:27,800
Yeah, yeah.

11
00:00:28,540 --> 00:00:28,800
Hard to believe, but true.

12
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:29,800
What?

13
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:30,260
I'll tell you.

14
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:31,000
I'll tell you all about it later.

15
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:32,800
Okay.

16
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:34,620
Well, the currency of America is also the US dollar.

17
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:39,800
So with that in mind, let's listen to today's dialogue.

18
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:44,600
Hey, John.

19
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:46,220
I haven't seen you in ages.

20
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:47,400
What's new?

21
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:48,880
What have you been up to?

22
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:50,840
Pete, nice to see you.

23
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:57,360
Well, on top of the norm, you know, life and kids and work, I've actually gotten into doing some trading.

24
00:00:57,880 --> 00:00:58,300
Trading?

25
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:00,480
You a big guy?

26
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:01,120
What are you trading?

27
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:03,160
Currencies.

28
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:04,200
Currencies?

29
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,840
As in euros, dollars, pounds and rupees?

30
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:09,320
It's called Forex, foreign exchange.

31
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:13,600
The great thing about it is that I don't have to invest a huge amount.

32
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:18,500
I put in a margin deposit and then I can buy and sell up to a hundred times that much.

33
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:19,840
I don't understand.

34
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:21,940
You're buying and selling money?

35
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:23,100
You got it.

36
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:27,000
Just last night, I made 150 US dollars.

37
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Last night?

38
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Yeah.

39
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,000
It's a 24-hour market.

40
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,680
I bought some RMB earlier at a low asking price, but last night it appreciated drastically.

41
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:40,080
So I made a split second decision and sold all of my RMB at an amazing bid.

42
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:45,020
I've also done some trading with Swiss francs and Australian dollars and Hong Kong dollars.

43
00:01:45,580 --> 00:01:48,080
I've made some good profits, but I've also suffered some losses.

44
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:51,300
It depends on a lot of factors, just like any other market.

45
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:55,180
In total, I've made about 500 US dollars in the past few months.

46
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:56,100
You're kidding.

47
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:57,240
I'm on.

48
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:00,320
I'm going to sign up.

49
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:03,440
Alright, we're back.

50
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:06,810
So this guy is trading currencies as we heard.

51
00:02:07,510 --> 00:02:13,010
And well, we're going to talk about a lot of the specific vocab that he used about this topic.

52
00:02:13,390 --> 00:02:16,450
So why don't we take a look at some of those now in Language Takeaway.

53
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:19,860
Language Takeaway.

54
00:02:20,810 --> 00:02:28,030
Well, the first thing I think we should talk about is, one of the first things that he says actually, Peter says, I haven't seen you in ages.

55
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:30,130
I haven't seen you in ages.

56
00:02:30,130 --> 00:02:30,550
Right.

57
00:02:31,130 --> 00:02:35,670
So obviously he hasn't seen him in a long time and that's what in ages means.

58
00:02:36,290 --> 00:02:36,730
That's right.

59
00:02:36,990 --> 00:02:39,290
So we haven't seen each other in ages.

60
00:02:40,170 --> 00:02:45,280
So what other ways can we use in ages or the word ages?

61
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:47,900
Well you could actually say, it's been ages.

62
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:49,920
So that means it's been a long time.

63
00:02:49,980 --> 00:02:53,760
It's been a long time or we haven't gone out for ages.

64
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:57,200
Okay, so then you can use it with for, for ages.

65
00:02:57,600 --> 00:02:59,640
For ages, in ages.

66
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:02,240
So the key word here is ages which means a long time.

67
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:02,820
Long time.

68
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:03,400
Okay.

69
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:07,980
And well, he hasn't seen him in ages and the guy is like, well, you know what?

70
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:10,120
I've actually gone into trading.

71
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:13,340
So that means he started doing some trading.

72
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:14,180
Right.

73
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:22,120
Now trading is a very broad word because you can trade as in this case, currencies which is basically to buy and sell currencies.

74
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:23,680
But you could also trade stocks, right?

75
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:28,400
You can trade stocks or even people that are involved in import export.

76
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:30,400
They are involved in trading.

77
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:32,940
Trading so basically trading is buying and selling something.

78
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:33,380
Right.

79
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:34,080
So you're trading.

80
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:38,180
You are exchanging a product for money or money for a product.

81
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:40,680
But the difference is you're not actually making any products.

82
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:41,300
Right.

83
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:43,360
Okay.

84
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:44,360
So that's, that's called trading.

85
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:45,840
You're not really manufacturing anything.

86
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:47,960
You're just exchanging goods and services and money.

87
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:48,960
All right.

88
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:50,940
So that's for trading.

89
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:58,340
Now moving on, he was explaining how the foreign exchange market works and he says, I don't have to invest a huge amount.

90
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:02,960
I put in a margin deposit and then I can buy and sell up to a hundred times that much.

91
00:04:03,420 --> 00:04:04,380
So Marco, here's the question.

92
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:06,360
What is a margin deposit?

93
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:12,240
Because a deposit is basically money that you put, invest somewhere or that you pay in.

94
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:12,700
Right.

95
00:04:13,180 --> 00:04:16,000
Well, basically a margin deposit is like a percentage.

96
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:21,640
So he basically puts in a percentage of what he intends to buy.

97
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:27,840
So for example, a margin deposit of a thousand dollars might be $1 or $10.

98
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:28,840
Okay.

99
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:31,540
So maybe if it's $10, you're talking about a 1% margin.

100
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:32,920
Exactly.

101
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:35,440
So that's what he means by a margin deposit.

102
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:43,200
And that's why he explains that you don't have to have a large amount of money in order to trade in the foreign exchange market.

103
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:46,960
You just put in a margin deposit and then you can work around that.

104
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:52,860
Well, it sounds pretty easy, but then he explains some more details about it and it gets to be a little bit more complicated.

105
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:53,560
That's right.

106
00:04:53,640 --> 00:05:02,480
He explained about how he makes money and how he bought some Chinese renminbi and then he sold it because it appreciated drastically.

107
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:06,500
So he bought it when it was cheap and he sold it when it was expensive.

108
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:07,880
So the price changed.

109
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:08,580
Right.

110
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:14,440
So when you see this kind of price change, when something goes from cheap to expensive, it's called appreciating.

111
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:14,860
Appreciation.

112
00:05:15,540 --> 00:05:17,900
So the verb is to appreciate.

113
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:22,560
It's very different from using it in the sense of you appreciate someone, right?

114
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:24,060
Like I really appreciate you.

115
00:05:24,310 --> 00:05:25,230
Yeah, no, it's very different.

116
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:28,360
We could say this about most goods or investments.

117
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:36,720
For example, my parents bought a house in 1970 and the value of the house has really appreciated over the last 40 years.

118
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:38,230
So now the value is much, much greater.

119
00:05:39,470 --> 00:05:45,850
And actually just mentioning it while we're on the topic, to appreciate, as you say, is something becomes more expensive.

120
00:05:46,690 --> 00:05:51,590
The opposite when something becomes cheaper or maybe the value goes down is to depreciate.

121
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:53,260
Depreciate.

122
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:55,380
So that D at the beginning means it's going down.

123
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:57,120
Right.

124
00:05:57,120 --> 00:05:58,120
Okay.

125
00:05:58,120 --> 00:06:00,260
And moving on to our last word for language takeaway.

126
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:07,700
So when he was talking about selling the Chinese currency, he said that he sold it at an amazing bid.

127
00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:10,080
Okay, let's talk about bid here.

128
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:11,520
B-I-D.

129
00:06:11,520 --> 00:06:12,520
Bid.

130
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:13,520
What does this mean?

131
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:16,040
So to bid is to place an offer for something.

132
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:17,040
Okay.

133
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:23,860
So for example, at an auction, when someone is selling many old objects or some art, I can bid $100.

134
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:28,200
And maybe someone doesn't accept my bid.

135
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:31,000
But if they do accept my bid, then I pay for it.

136
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Right.

137
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,380
So basically a bid is just another way of saying an offer.

138
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:39,640
You're offering a certain amount of money for that product or that service.

139
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:42,160
And you see it a lot, for example, on eBay, right?

140
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:43,160
That's right.

141
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:46,160
So eBay is an auction website basically.

142
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:51,080
And someone who's selling something, someone can see many, many bids and they select the best bid.

143
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:52,080
Exactly.

144
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:53,840
So that's what a bid is.

145
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:56,080
So that's all we have for language takeaway.

146
00:06:56,080 --> 00:07:01,280
Let's move on now to fluency builder.

147
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:02,280
Fluency builder.

148
00:07:02,930 --> 00:07:04,510
Marco, what have you been up to lately?

149
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:05,640
I never see you.

150
00:07:05,970 --> 00:07:06,210
Right.

151
00:07:06,710 --> 00:07:09,510
This is actually a very common sentence.

152
00:07:10,350 --> 00:07:11,190
What have you been up to?

153
00:07:11,450 --> 00:07:14,410
But most English learners don't really understand what it means.

154
00:07:14,950 --> 00:07:18,490
Well, this is a really interesting point because I say this all the time.

155
00:07:18,710 --> 00:07:20,910
This is very native, very colloquial.

156
00:07:21,450 --> 00:07:23,070
What have you been up to?

157
00:07:23,450 --> 00:07:24,810
It's almost the same as saying what's up.

158
00:07:25,150 --> 00:07:25,690
What's up?

159
00:07:25,690 --> 00:07:27,530
Or where have you been recently?

160
00:07:27,960 --> 00:07:30,560
What have you been doing recently?

161
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:31,010
How's your life?

162
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:31,870
Right.

163
00:07:32,560 --> 00:07:35,070
So this part, up to, what have you been up to?

164
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:38,890
It doesn't really say much if you translate it literally.

165
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:42,940
But basically it's what have you been doing?

166
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:45,170
What have you been spending your time on?

167
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:48,130
So to be up to means to do something, to do.

168
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:49,720
Right.

169
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:53,470
And there are many other definitions of up to, but in this case, what have you been up to means that.

170
00:07:53,940 --> 00:07:54,370
What have you been doing?

171
00:07:54,940 --> 00:07:59,330
So the next time you see a friend you haven't seen in a week or two, you can say, what have you been up to?

172
00:08:00,430 --> 00:08:01,070
All right.

173
00:08:01,960 --> 00:08:06,490
And John answers, well, you know, on top of the norm, wife, kids, and work.

174
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:09,230
That phrase right there, on top of the norm.

175
00:08:09,810 --> 00:08:11,450
Well, the norm here means the normal.

176
00:08:11,890 --> 00:08:14,310
That means basically normal life.

177
00:08:14,970 --> 00:08:16,430
I've been working.

178
00:08:16,850 --> 00:08:19,430
I've got my kids, my wife, on top of the norm.

179
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:21,200
Just life is normal.

180
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:22,200
Right.

181
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:23,900
So the norm are the wife, kids, and work.

182
00:08:24,320 --> 00:08:26,860
On top of that, I'm actually gotten into trading.

183
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:27,480
Right.

184
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:32,020
So the normal situation, the normal life is wife, kids, and work.

185
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:34,940
And then he's saying on top of that, I've actually been doing this.

186
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:36,880
Exactly.

187
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:37,880
Okay.

188
00:08:37,880 --> 00:08:46,940
And now moving on to when he's explaining again, the market and the currencies, he talked about an asking price.

189
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:47,850
Okay.

190
00:08:47,990 --> 00:08:48,750
Asking price.

191
00:08:49,560 --> 00:08:52,200
This is, remember we were talking about bids.

192
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:59,800
We could say that someone has an asking price, which is the price they would like to receive for an object or a service that they are selling.

193
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:02,080
And so for example, I'm going to sell my desk.

194
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:05,920
My asking price is a hundred bucks, a hundred dollars.

195
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:09,400
Maybe you don't want to pay that much, but my asking price is the way that I'm going to start.

196
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:10,520
That's how much I want for it.

197
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:12,080
Right.

198
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:13,080
So that's your asking price.

199
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:16,620
That's how much you ideally want to sell it for, but you can negotiate.

200
00:09:16,960 --> 00:09:19,020
You are willing to accept maybe a lower offer.

201
00:09:19,320 --> 00:09:19,500
Exactly.

202
00:09:20,120 --> 00:09:20,320
Okay.

203
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:23,160
And what is our last phrase?

204
00:09:23,160 --> 00:09:24,620
A split second decision.

205
00:09:25,120 --> 00:09:26,140
This is really, really important.

206
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:27,900
These words go together very often.

207
00:09:28,380 --> 00:09:30,860
Split second and decision.

208
00:09:31,560 --> 00:09:31,860
Okay.

209
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:34,500
So that basically means that you made a very quick decision.

210
00:09:34,980 --> 00:09:35,220
That's right.

211
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:37,160
Split second.

212
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:38,160
Very fast.

213
00:09:38,160 --> 00:09:39,480
It's almost like it's less than a second.

214
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:43,640
And so a split second decision means a decision in no time.

215
00:09:43,640 --> 00:09:44,800
It's very, very quick.

216
00:09:45,220 --> 00:09:48,280
So we can actually mix split second with other things, right?

217
00:09:48,900 --> 00:09:49,380
For example.

218
00:09:49,900 --> 00:09:53,420
Maybe you can say he reacted in a split second and avoided the crash.

219
00:09:54,100 --> 00:09:54,380
Okay.

220
00:09:54,460 --> 00:09:55,160
So good for him.

221
00:09:55,400 --> 00:09:58,560
He reacted quickly enough to avoid the accident.

222
00:09:58,920 --> 00:09:59,200
That's right.

223
00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:02,120
So split second, very fast, very quickly.

224
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:05,260
A split second decision, a decision that was made very fast.

225
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:06,340
Okay.

226
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:08,200
So that's all we have for Fluency Builder.

227
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:12,820
Why don't we go back, listen to this dialogue one more time and we'll be back to talk a little bit more.

228
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:22,420
Hey John, I haven't seen you in ages.

229
00:10:23,210 --> 00:10:23,550
What's new?

230
00:10:24,220 --> 00:10:25,120
What have you been up to?

231
00:10:25,550 --> 00:10:26,990
Pete, nice to see you.

232
00:10:27,370 --> 00:10:33,550
Well, on top of the norm, you know, life and kids and work, I've actually gotten into doing some trading.

233
00:10:34,090 --> 00:10:34,410
Trading?

234
00:10:35,570 --> 00:10:36,110
You big guy?

235
00:10:36,610 --> 00:10:37,290
What are you trading?

236
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:39,320
Currencies.

237
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:40,320
Currencies?

238
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:42,910
As in euros, dollars, pounds and rupees?

239
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:45,510
It's called Forex, foreign exchange.

240
00:10:46,490 --> 00:10:49,790
The great thing about it is that I don't have to invest a huge amount.

241
00:10:50,490 --> 00:10:54,650
I put in a margin deposit and then I can buy and sell up to a hundred times that much.

242
00:10:55,260 --> 00:10:56,020
I don't understand.

243
00:10:56,880 --> 00:10:58,120
You're buying and selling money?

244
00:10:58,700 --> 00:10:59,280
You got it.

245
00:11:00,040 --> 00:11:01,820
Just last night I made $150 US.

246
00:11:03,160 --> 00:11:03,340
Last night?

247
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:05,160
Yeah.

248
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:05,540
It's a 24 hour market.

249
00:11:06,160 --> 00:11:11,880
I'd bought some RMB earlier at a low asking price, but last night it appreciated drastically.

250
00:11:11,880 --> 00:11:16,260
So I made a split second decision and sold all of my RMB at an amazing bid.

251
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:21,180
I've also done some trading with Swiss francs and Australian dollars and Hong Kong dollars.

252
00:11:21,820 --> 00:11:24,280
I've made some good profits, but I've also suffered some losses.

253
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:28,240
It depends on a lot of factors, just like any other market.

254
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:31,360
In total I've made about $500 US in the past few months.

255
00:11:31,960 --> 00:11:32,260
You're kidding!

256
00:11:33,100 --> 00:11:33,420
I'm on.

